<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Pagania</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1050 CE (approx.) · capital Omiš</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Slavic principality of the Narentines between the Cetina and Neretva, whose pirate fleets challenged Venice for two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Narentines were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> who settled the coast between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetina">Cetina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neretva">Neretva</a> rivers in the 7th century. By the 9th century they formed a principality, called Pagania by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> because they accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> late. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> lists its towns, including Mokro and Ostrog, and the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brac">Brač</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hvar">Hvar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korcula">Korčula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mljet">Mljet</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ruled by its own princes and assembly of clans, Pagania lived largely from the sea, raiding and taxing Adriatic shipping. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> had to pay for safe passage, and in 887 the Narentines killed Doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-i-candiano">Pietro I Candiano</a> in battle. They accepted Byzantine missionaries in the late 9th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1000 Doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-ii-orseolo">Pietro II Orseolo</a> led a fleet that broke Narentine sea power. Pagania then came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-925-1102">Kingdom of Croatia</a> (925-1102) and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zachlumia">Zachlumia</a>, and the name fades by the 11th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pagania.jpg" alt="Pagania" width="320" height="238"><figcaption>Image: User:PANONIAN, derivative work: User:Ceha · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neretva9st.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1050 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Omiš</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Slavic paganism; Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Croatian (Slavonic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Croatia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Narentines, Principality of the Narentines, Neretva Principality</dd></dl></aside></div>
